    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a>'s dialogue asks whether the just man is happier than the unjust man, and builds a hypothetical city, Kallipolis, ruled by philosopher-kings, to see how justice comes to be in the soul.</li><li>It compares five kinds of regime, aristocracy, timocracy, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oligarchy">oligarchy</a>, democracy and tyranny, and the kinds of soul that correspond to them.</li><li>Readers have disagreed from the 20th century onward over whether <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a> meant the city as a political programme, an ironic exercise or an analogy for the individual soul.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The English title comes from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cicero">Cicero</a>'s De re publica, written about three centuries later, and is not an exact translation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a>'s Greek title Politeia, a general term for the actual and possible forms of government of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-state</a>.</p></aside>
    </section>

