    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">c. 375 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a> writes the Republic, a dialogue on justice, the just <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-state</a> and the just man</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 200</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxyrhynchus">Oxyrhynchus</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papyrus">papyrus</a> fragments containing parts of the Republic are written, between about 200 and 300 CE</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 350</span><span class="rf-text">A different version of the Republic is copied in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nag-hammadi-library">Nag Hammadi library</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1871</span><span class="rf-text">Benjamin Jowett publishes his translation</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1902</span><span class="rf-text">John Burnet publishes the Greek text in his Platonis opera</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1945</span><span class="rf-text">Karl Popper's The Open Society and Its Enemies singles out <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a>'s state as a dystopia, and fragments of a different version are discovered at Nag Hammadi</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">2003</span><span class="rf-text">Simon R. Slings publishes a new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a> edition of the Greek text</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/plato"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/plato.jpg" alt="Plato" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Plato_Silanion_Musei_Capitolini_MC1377.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Marie-Lan Nguyen, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Republic (Ancient Greek Politeia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> De Republica) is a Socratic dialogue that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a> wrote around 375 BCE. It concerns justice (dikaiosune), the order and character of the just <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-state</a>, and the just man. Its setting seems to be the time of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnesian-war">Peloponnesian War</a>. The conversation begins when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/socrates">Socrates</a> and Glaucon visit <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>' port, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piraeus">Piraeus</a>, and are intercepted by Polemarchus, who demands that they join him for a dinner and festival. At Polemarchus' house Socrates meets Polemarchus' father, Cephalus.</p><p>The work is generally placed in the middle period of Plato's dialogues, although the line between the middle and early dialogues is less clear than the line between the late dialogues and all the others. Ritter, Arnim and Baron, each using a separate method, agree that the Republic stood apart, along with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parmenides">Parmenides</a>, Phaedrus and Theaetetus. The first book shares many features with earlier dialogues and is thought to have been written first as a separate work, with the remaining books joined to it, perhaps with changes to the first book.</p><p>The English title derives from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cicero">Cicero</a>'s De re publica, but res publica is not an exact translation of politeia, a general term for the actual and potential forms of government for a polis.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="content">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What It Is</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/socrates"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/socrates.jpg" alt="Socrates" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/socrates">Socrates</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Socrates_Louvre.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Copy of Lysippos (?), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In Book I <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/socrates">Socrates</a> asks Cephalus about old age and the definition of justice; Cephalus holds that justice is honesty and returning what is owed, Polemarchus that it is doing good to friends and harm to enemies, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrasymachus">Thrasymachus</a> that it is whatever is advantageous for the stronger. Socrates answers each in turn. In Book II Glaucon tells the story of Gyges, who uses a ring that makes him invisible to gain advantages by injustice, and asks Socrates to defend justice as good in itself. Socrates proposes to use a city as an image of the soul, and describes a healthy city of producers, then a luxurious one that needs a guardian class.</p><p>The later books set out Kallipolis, the just city. The guardians receive a censored education, are forbidden private property, and are told a noble lie, the myth of the metals. Justice is defined as each class doing its own work, matched by a soul of rational, spirited and appetitive parts. Philosophers, who know the Forms, are to rule; the Sun, the Divided Line and the allegory of the cave illustrate the Form of the Good. Book VIII traces decline from aristocracy through timocracy, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oligarchy">oligarchy</a> and democracy to tyranny, and Book X ends with the myth of Er.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="making">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Was Made</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Republic is a dialogue, in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/socrates">Socrates</a> discusses justice with various Athenians and foreigners. It is divided into ten books, and the passages are cited by the numbers of the Stephanus pagination, such as 327a for the start of Book I and 621d for the end of Book X. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a> wrote it around 375 BCE. The first book is thought to have been composed as a separate work and joined to the rest, perhaps with changes.</p><p>Its arrangement has been described in different ways. Eric Voegelin saw an introduction and conclusion that frame a main body, with the Prologue in Book I, a short dialogue on common opinions about justice, and the Epilogue in Book X, which he said rests on faith and not reason and describes the new arts and the immortality of the soul. The main body, in his reading, has three parts, the establishment of the economic and social orders of a polis, the embodiment of the idea of the city, and the analysis of its decline.</p><p>Within the text Socrates works by refutation and by analogy: he answers each definition of justice in Book I with a counter-example or argument, and later offers images such as the Ship of State, the Sun, the Divided Line and the cave. In Book IV he sets the parts of the city beside the parts of the soul, and in Book VIII Plato's number is mentioned enigmatically.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="reception">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Reception</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/aristotle"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aristotle.jpg" alt="Aristotle" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aristotle_Altemps_Inv8575.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">After Lysippos, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a> systematised many of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a>'s analyses in his Politics and criticised the proposals of several political philosophers for the ideal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-state</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeno-of-citium">Zeno of Citium</a>, the founder of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stoicism">Stoicism</a>, wrote a Republic of his own in opposition to Plato's, which some later Stoics viewed with embarrassment because of its defences of free love, incest and cannibalism. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cicero">Cicero</a>'s De re publica, about three centuries later, imitates Plato's style and treats many of the same topics, though its discussion centres more on improving the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augustine-of-hippo">Augustine of Hippo</a> described an ideal city of his own, the eternal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, in his City of God.</p><p>Islamic philosophers were more interested in Aristotle than in Plato, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/averroes">Ibn Rushd</a> (Averroes), without access to Aristotle's Politics, wrote a commentary on the Republic. He took up its ideal of a monarchy led by a philosopher-king, its equality of women, who should be educated and serve in the military, and its censorship of literature. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-more">Thomas More</a>'s Utopia shares with the Republic common property and the lack of privacy. Hegel held that Plato had grasped the spirit of his own time and wrote the Republic as an attempt to keep out the new freedom of private property.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="survival">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Survival &amp; Rediscovery</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/nag-hammadi-library"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nag-hammadi-library.jpg" alt="Nag Hammadi library" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/nag-hammadi-library">Nag Hammadi library</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:El_Evangelio_de_Tomás-Gospel_of_Thomas-_Codex_II_Manuscritos_de_Nag_Hammadi-The_Nag_Hammadi_manuscripts.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Several <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxyrhynchus">Oxyrhynchus</a> Papyri fragments were found to contain parts of the Republic, along with parts of other works such as Phaedo and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorgias">Gorgias</a>, written around 200 to 300 CE. Fragments of a different version of the Republic were discovered in 1945 as part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nag-hammadi-library">Nag Hammadi library</a>, written about 350 CE. These finds are taken to show the influence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in those times.</p><p>The work later reached English readers through translations, among them J. L. Davies and D. J. Vaughan in 1852, George Burges in 1854, Benjamin Jowett in 1871, H. D. P. Lee in 1955, Allan Bloom in 1991, Robin Waterfield in 1994, Tom Griffith in 2000, R. E. Allen in 2006, Joe Sachs in 2007 and Christopher Rowe in 2012. Jowett's translation is available as an e-text from Project Gutenberg, and Paul Shorey's of 1935 with the Greek on the Perseus Project. The standard Greek texts named include John Burnet's Platonis opera of 1902 and Simon R. Slings' edition of 2003. A public-domain audiobook of the Republic is available at LibriVox, and the Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy and the Internet Encyclopedia of Philosophy carry articles on it.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther-king-jr"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/martin-luther-king-jr.jpg" alt="Martin Luther King Jr." loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther-king-jr">Martin Luther King Jr.</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Martin_Luther_King,_Jr._and_Lyndon_Johnson_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Yoichi Okamoto, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Readers in the 20th century drew very different conclusions. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Mussolini</a> admired the Republic and often read it for inspiration, and it contains ideas that fascism promoted, such as rule by an elite and the militarisation of a nation through a class of warriors, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a> was an idealist focused on justice and morality, while fascism was realist, focused on political goals. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther-king-jr">Martin Luther King Jr.</a> named the Republic as the one book, apart from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a>, that he would want on a desert island.</p><p>Critics disagreed on its purpose. Karl Popper, in The Open Society and Its Enemies of 1945, called Plato's state a totalitarian dystopia and held that it was meant as a topical political manifesto; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bertrand-russell">Bertrand Russell</a> argued that it was meant as a practical form of government, and G. A. Cohen that it was not totalitarian in the strict sense. Gadamer, Leo Strauss and Allan Bloom read it as a heuristic utopia or a learning exercise, and Voegelin considered Popper's reading a gross misunderstanding. Others read the city as an analogy for the individual soul. Aldous Huxley's Brave New World has been compared to it, and Louis Andriessen composed De Staat on its text in the early 1970s.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Republic is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a>'s Socratic dialogue of about 375 BCE on justice, in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/socrates">Socrates</a> and others describe a hypothetical just city ruled by philosopher-kings and compare it with the soul. It was taken up by later philosophers and remains disputed as to its purpose.</em></p>

